== Mock Examination 1 — Complete Solutions

#sol-item(1, "D")[
  *Chapter 1.* The seedling increases in dry mass (growth) and uses stored food with oxygen uptake (respiration); light is not needed for these two.
]

#sol-item(2, "C")[
  *Chapter 2.* Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll; a cell with many of them is specialised for photosynthesis, which is why palisade cells sit just under the upper epidermis.
]

#sol-item(3, "B")[
  *Chapter 3.* At metaphase the chromosomes are at the equator — the classic 'on the plate' appearance.
]

#sol-item(4, "A")[
  *Chapter 4.* Below the compensation point respiration outruns photosynthesis, so the leaf shows a net uptake of oxygen and a net release of carbon dioxide.
]

#sol-item(5, "D")[
  *Chapter 5.* The ileum is long and lined with villi and microvilli — the absorptive surface area is enormous.
]

#sol-item(6, "B")[
  *Chapter 6.* Moving air removes the humid layer around the leaf, keeping the diffusion gradient steep.
]

#sol-item(7, "C")[
  *Chapter 7.* Right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs → pulmonary veins → left atrium.
]

#sol-item(8, "A")[
  *Chapter 8.* The extra oxygen taken in after exercise is used to break down the accumulated lactic acid.
]

#sol-item(9, "D")[
  *Chapter 9.* Sweat glands and the arterioles of the dermis are the two effectors controlled by the hypothalamus.
]

#sol-item(10, "B")[
  *Chapter 10.* Quadriceps extends the knee; hamstrings flex it. They form an antagonistic pair.
]

#sol-item(11, "C")[
  *Chapter 1.* Only the second word is the species name; it is written in small letters and italicised (or underlined in handwriting).
]

#sol-item(12, "A")[
  *Chapter 2.* Cell sap in the vacuole pushes the cytoplasm against the wall (turgor), which supports the cell and the whole plant.
]

#sol-item(13, "D")[
  *Chapter 3.* Growth by mitosis is clonal: every body cell keeps the same chromosome complement as the zygote.
]

#sol-item(14, "A")[
  *Chapter 4.* Magnesium sits at the centre of the chlorophyll molecule, which is why it is a component of chlorophyll.
]

#sol-item(15, "B")[
  *Chapter 5.* Pancreatic juice supplies trypsin (and chymotrypsin) for protein, amylase for starch and lipase for fat.
]

#sol-item(16, "C")[
  *Chapter 6.* Sunken stomata trap humid air next to the pore, and a thick cuticle is nearly waterproof; both cut transpirational loss.
]

#sol-item(17, "B")[
  *Chapter 7.* The atria contract first (P wave), then the ventricles (QRS); the T wave marks ventricular relaxation.
]

#sol-item(18, "A")[
  *Chapter 8.* Skeletal muscle working at maximum power has an enormous demand for ATP and therefore for oxygen.
]

#sol-item(19, "C")[
  *Chapter 9.* Sweating is a corrective response that restores the set point of temperature, so it is homeostasis in action.
]

#sol-item(20, "D")[
  *Chapter 10.* An internal bone can be enlarged by growth; an exoskeleton has to be shed, which is risky and limits growth.
]

#sol-item(21, "A")[
  *Chapter 1.* Measles is caused by a virus. Cholera, tuberculosis and tetanus are all bacterial diseases.
]

#sol-item(22, "B")[
  *Chapter 2.* Several organs with a common function form a system; the stomach is an organ within the digestive system.
]

#sol-item(23, "C")[
  *Chapter 3.* Meiosis I and II each divide the cell, so one diploid mother cell yields four haploid products.
]

#sol-item(24, "D")[
  *Chapter 4.* In darkness there is no light to be captured, so whatever the carbon dioxide level, photosynthesis cannot proceed.
]

#sol-item(25, "C")[
  *Chapter 5.* Cellulose walls must be broken mechanically before the gut enzymes can reach the contents.
]

#sol-item(26, "D")[
  *Chapter 6.* Translocation in the phloem carries sucrose and amino acids from sources to sinks, up or down.
]

#sol-item(27, "A")[
  *Chapter 7.* The atria are collecting chambers; the thick-walled ventricles do the pumping.
]

#sol-item(28, "B")[
  *Chapter 8.* Diffusion follows the concentration gradient, which is reversed for carbon dioxide at the alveolar surface.
]

#sol-item(29, "C")[
  *Chapter 9.* High pressure in the glomerulus forces water, glucose, salts and urea into the capsule, but not blood cells or large proteins.
]

#sol-item(30, "A")[
  *Chapter 10.* Oestrogen helps to maintain bone density; after the menopause bone loss speeds up and fractures become common.
]

#sol-item(31, "D")[
  *Chapter 1.* Kingdom → phylum → class → *order* → family → genus → species. A handy sentence: *King Philip Came Over For Good Soup*.
]

#sol-item(32, "B")[
  *Chapter 2.* A long thin extension adds surface area without adding much volume, so absorption per unit volume rises.
]

#sol-item(33, "A")[
  *Chapter 3.* After meiosis I the chromosome number is halved (12) but each chromosome is still duplicated (2 chromatids).
]

#sol-item(34, "C")[
  *Chapter 4.* Only the green regions can make starch; the white regions show that the result depends on chlorophyll.
]

#sol-item(35, "D")[
  *Chapter 5.* Material that never entered the body's cells is egested, not excreted.
]

#sol-item(36, "B")[
  *Chapter 6.* Ions absorbed by the root are carried upwards in the xylem sap, as well as redistributed in the phloem.
]

#sol-item(37, "D")[
  *Chapter 7.* These are the standard features used to identify a vein in a diagram question.
]

#sol-item(38, "B")[
  *Chapter 8.* Soda lime absorbs the carbon dioxide produced, so any volume change in the flask is due to oxygen uptake.
]

#sol-item(39, "A")[
  *Chapter 9.* A long loop builds a steeper osmotic gradient in the medulla, so more water can be recovered.
]

#sol-item(40, "C")[
  *Chapter 10.* Collenchyma has thickened cellulose at the corners, which supports growing stems without stopping growth.
]

